data stick_capacity_bytes_16g: ByteSize = byte_size(17179869184)

// MTA36ADS2G72PZ-2G1A1, DECODED FIELD BY FIELD AGAINST THE NUMBERING SHEET, then checked
// against the SPD image Micron publishes for it. Numbering sheet, Rev. 05-Dec-2018:
//   MT     Micron Technology
//   A      Product Family: DDR4 SDRAM
//   36     Number of Die
//   A      Voltage: 1.2V
//   DS     Module Options: "VLP Dual-Die w/Temp Sensor"
//   2G72   Module Configuration, depth x width: 2 Gig x 72 (G = Gigabits)
//   P      Module Type: 288-pin RDIMM
//   Z      Package Code: commercial temp, halogen-free, single-/dual-/quad-/octal-rank DIMM
//   -2G1   Module Speed: DDR4-2133, PC4-2133, SPD CL-nRCD-nRP 15-15-15, component mark -093E
//   A      Die Revision
//   1      Printed Circuit Board Revision
// So the family letters answer the brief's second question outright: the sibling
// MTA36ASF2G72PZ differs ONLY in the module-options field, SF = "FBGA w/Temp Sensor" -- a
// full-height module built from single-die FBGA packages -- where DS is a very-low-profile
// module built from dual-die packages. D is the height, S is the package; the operator's
// reading of the label "2DRx4" as "two ranks, dual-die, x4" is what Micron's own code says.
//
// THE SPD IMAGE AGREES AT EVERY BYTE THE ROW READS (JESD21-C Annex L byte layout; process
// code JG, one of twelve the record lists, which differ only in register-vendor bytes 133-135
// and the bytes-128..253 CRC and NOT in any organization byte):
//   byte 2  = 0x0C  DDR4 SDRAM
//   byte 3  = 0x01  module type RDIMM
//   byte 4  = 0x84  4 Gb per die, 4 bank groups x 4 banks
//   byte 5  = 0x21  16 row address bits, 10 column bits
//   byte 6  = 0x91  NON-MONOLITHIC, 2 dies, MULTI LOAD STACK -- the dual-die package
//   byte 12 = 0x08  2 package ranks, x4 device width, symmetrical
//   byte 13 = 0x0B  64-bit primary bus + 8-bit ECC extension
//   byte 18 = 0x08 with byte 125 = 0xC2 (-62 ps)  tCKmin 938 ps = DDR4-2133
//   byte 24 = 0x6C  tAAmin 13.5 ns -> CL 15 at 2133
//   byte 128 = 0x04  module nominal height <= 19 mm (the VLP outline; the ASF sibling reads
//                    0x11, <= 32 mm, the full-height outline)
//   byte 130 = 0x08  raw card reference design byte differs from the sibling's 0x01
//   bytes 329-349     "36ADS2G72PZ-2G1A1"; bytes 320-321 = 0x80 0x2C, JEP106 Micron
// The catalogue details table says the same in words: Density 16GB, Industry Speed PC4-2133,
// 2133 MT/s, CAS Latency CL = 15, Bus Width x72, ECC True, Component Config "2Gb x4" (that is
// the 8 Gb DUAL-DIE PACKAGE: 2 Gig x 4 = 8 Gbit per package, two 4 Gb dies), Number of
// Components 36 (dies, matching the part-number field), Package FBGA, Part Status Obsolete.
//
// WHAT THE ROW CARRIES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. rank_count is the module's package-rank
// organization, 2, exactly as for a monolithic 2Rx4 -- byte 12 counts CS-selected ranks and a
// DDP's second die is CS1-selected. dram_module_required_die_density divides 16 GiB by (16
// data dies x 2 ranks) and lands on 4 Gbit, which byte 4 states independently, so three row
// figures corroborate a fourth the way the Samsung row's do. The 18.75 mm outline is a
// FORM-FACTOR fact the catalogue row type has no field for; it is stated here and in the
// numbering decode, and becomes a typed carrier when a chassis DIMM-clearance consumer exists
// to read it (DESIGN 3c: no dangling height row).
//
// The eBay listing that sold these modules described them as "2Rx4"; the physical label reads
// "2DRx4". The listing is not a source for any field of this row.
//
// INDEPENDENTLY MEASURED, NOT AUTHORED FROM HERE: the Mt. Collins unit-1 host firmware prints
// this part as "RDIMM[2c:80] 16GB 2133 ECC 2R x4 RCD[b3:80] 36ADS2G72PZ-2G1A1" and, when a
// socket also held monolithic modules, "CHANNEL Mismatch Byte 6 SLOT0[00] EXP[91]" -- so the
// module's own SPD reads byte 6 = 0x91 on the board, agreeing with the image Micron publishes.
// That reading is a receipt in the observing layer (hw-first-host, 2026-09-12) and is quoted
// here only as the corroboration; the row cites Micron.
data mta36ads2g72pz_2g1a1_catalog: DramModuleCatalogRow = DramModuleCatalogRow {
  part_number: "MTA36ADS2G72PZ-2G1A1",
  manufacturer: micron,
  generation: dram_generation_ddr4,
  capacity_bytes: stick_capacity_bytes_16g,
  data_rate_mts: 2133,
  ecc: true,
  buffering: Registered,
  die_stacking: DualDiePackage,
  chip_width: bit_width(4),

  rank_count: 2,
}

data mta36ads2g72pz_2g1a1_catalog_citation: FactCitation = FactCitation {
  fact: decl_ref(module_path: "extdeps.memory.micron", decl_name: "mta36ads2g72pz_2g1a1_catalog"),
  authority: micron_part_catalog_mta36ads2g72pz_2g1a1,
}

// The numbering sheet attests the part-number decode above (DS = VLP dual-die, -2G1 = 2133
// CL15), which the catalogue record confirms but does not explain. Two citations for one row
// because they ground different fields of it, not because either is weak.
data mta36ads2g72pz_2g1a1_numbering_citation: FactCitation = FactCitation {
  fact: decl_ref(module_path: "extdeps.memory.micron", decl_name: "mta36ads2g72pz_2g1a1_catalog"),
  authority: micron_ddr4_module_part_numbering_rev_2018_12_05,
}

// MTA36ASF2G72PZ-2G1A2: THE CONTROL, AND THE ONLY AXES ON WHICH IT DIFFERS ARE THE POINT.
// Same numbering decode except SF = "FBGA w/Temp Sensor", same 36 dies, same 2G72, same P, Z,
// -2G1; die revision A, PCB revision 2. Micron's SPD for it (process code KI; catalogue path
// obsolete-rdimm) reads byte 4 = 0x84, byte 5 = 0x21, byte 12 = 0x08, byte 13 = 0x0B, byte
// 18 = 0x08 / byte 125 = 0xC2, byte 24 = 0x6C -- identical to the ADS part at every
// organization and timing byte -- and differs at byte 6 = 0x00 (MONOLITHIC), byte 128 = 0x11
// (<= 32 mm), byte 130 = 0x01 (raw card), byte 131 = 0x09 versus 0x05. Details table:
// Component Config "1Gb x4" (a 4 Gb monolithic package), Number of Components 36.
//
// So Micron's own data says the two quartets the operator ran share vendor, generation,
// capacity, data rate, CAS latency, ECC, buffering, device width, rank count AND die density.
// What differs is the package construction (dual-die multi-load stack versus monolithic), the
// PCB outline (18.75 mm versus 31.25 mm) and, with it, the raw-card reference design. That is
// the whole of what these two rows can say; which of those three the Mt. Collins training
// outcome tracks is an observation for the observing layer and is not authored here.
//
// THE SUFFIX IS MICRON'S, AND THE LABEL AGREES. -2G1A2 is the MTA36ASF2G72PZ-2G1 SKU Micron's
// catalogue serves a record for. The Mt. Collins unit-1 host firmware reads the trained
// quartet's SPD part-number field as 36ASF2G72PZ-2G1A2II (hw-first-host SOL capture,
// 2026-09-12): the same die/PCB revision pair, followed by II, which is one of the eight
// process codes Micron's record lists for this SKU (KI/KJ/KK/KG/II/IG/IJ/IK). A process code
// selects register-vendor bytes and nothing organizational, so the row's part_number stays at
// the catalogue grain and the process code is a per-module observation.
data mta36asf2g72pz_2g1a2_catalog: DramModuleCatalogRow = DramModuleCatalogRow {
  part_number: "MTA36ASF2G72PZ-2G1A2",
  manufacturer: micron,
  generation: dram_generation_ddr4,
  capacity_bytes: stick_capacity_bytes_16g,
  data_rate_mts: 2133,
  ecc: true,
  buffering: Registered,
  die_stacking: MonolithicDie,
  chip_width: bit_width(4),

  rank_count: 2,
}

data mta36asf2g72pz_2g1a2_catalog_citation: FactCitation = FactCitation {
  fact: decl_ref(module_path: "extdeps.memory.micron", decl_name: "mta36asf2g72pz_2g1a2_catalog"),
  authority: micron_part_catalog_mta36asf2g72pz_2g1a2,
}

